<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="so3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>SO3: The Laplace Transform (Second-Order Equations)</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use the Laplace transform to solve second-order, linear differential equations.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
   <subsection xml:id="sec-laplace-second-order-deriv">
    <title>The Laplace Transform for Second-Order Derivatives</title>
        <assemblage>
            <title>Laplace Transform of a Second Derivative</title>
            <p>
                Using the techniques developed in <xref ref="sec-laplace-transform-solving-first-order"/>, it can be shown that, given a function <m>f(t)</m>,
                <md>
                    \mathcal{L}(f''(t)) = s^2F(s) - sf(0) - f'(0)
                </md>
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    We'll use the Laplace transform to solve the equation
                    <md>
                        y'' - 2y' + 5y = -5
                    </md>
                    with <m>y(0) = 0</m> and <m>y'(0) = -3</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Transform each component:
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}(y'') = s^2Y(s) - sy(0) - y'(0) = s^2 Y(s) + 3
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}(y') = sY(s) - y(0) = sY(s)
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}(y) = Y(s)
                    </md>
                    so that we have
                    <md>
                        s^2Y(s) + 3 - 2sY(s) + 5Y(s) = \dfrac{-5}{s}
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = -\dfrac{3}{s^2-2s+5} + \dfrac{-5}{s(s^2-2s+5)}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Decomposing and working the algebra gives
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = -\dfrac{1}{s} + \dfrac{s-1}{(s-1)^2 + 4} - 2\cdot \dfrac{2}{(s-1)^2 + 4}
                    </md>
                    which, after comparing to <xref ref="table-laplace-transforms-first"/>, gives the solution
                    <md>
                        y(t) = -1 + e^t[\cos (2t) - 2\sin(2t)]
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
   </subsection>
</section>